From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 06:56:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Let the camera power
 the ISP

Take the J713 camera power policy for the J604: enable the ISP's own
power states under ISP_SYS (CPU, CPU cores 0 and 1, FE, DPRX, secure,
STS0, STS1, Pearl, BE, CLR, CVD, CVM) and the two camera sensor power
states ISPSENS0/1, as aurora-silicon/linux 47d8f59220 and 8295d7f019 do
in t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi.

The J604 ADT's PMGR device table is byte-identical to the J713's in the
25G83 templates (392 entries, no differing device); the 26B123 J604
table only adds DISP-SYS-V. The ISP states are therefore at the same
offsets with the same parents as on the J713. ISP_SYS was already
enabled for genpd to power the unused ISP down; the comment now says who
uses it.

Nothing consumes these domains until the ISP node is enabled, which the
boot loader does after its firmware handoff (t8132-j604-camera.dtsi).

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi
index b55d2e97aa..24cca4fdba 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi
@@ -404,7 +404,7 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
-/* No camera driver yet; genpd powers the ISP down as an unused domain. */
+/* The camera powers the ISP from its first stream until it is unbound. */
 &ps_isp_sys {
 	status = "okay";
 };
